<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Defiance Campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1952">1952</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws was a campaign against apartheid, presented by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> in December 1951 and launched on 26 June 1952. It was the first large-scale, multi-racial political mobilisation against apartheid laws under a common leadership: volunteers burned their pass books, entered places reserved for whites and refused to defend themselves in court. Over 2,000 people were arrested in October 1952 alone, and on 9 November police in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> killed 14 protesters. The government answered with stiff penalties and the Public Safety Act, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli">Albert Luthuli</a> called the campaign off in April 1953.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in 1923, was founded at Bloemfontein in 1912; Clements Kadalie’s Industrial and Commercial Workers’ Union organised tens of thousands of workers in the 1920s; and in the 1950s the ANC and its allies ran the <b>Defiance Campaign</b> of 1952 and adopted the Freedom Charter at Kliptown in 1955.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1952 the ANC and the South African Indian Congress launched the <b>Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws</b>: volunteers deliberately broke segregation laws, entering “whites only” facilities and defying curfews, and more than 8,000 were arrested.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
